WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.686 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Does the word more always tell you to add?

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:07.109 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are modelling addition and subtraction problems.

00:00:07.329 --> 00:00:12.835 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will identify the quantities, decide how they relate, and
check both the number and the unit.

00:00:13.055 --> 00:00:16.658 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The story, rather than one keyword, tells us what to do.

00:00:17.308 --> 00:00:22.999 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to model everyday addition and
subtraction problems and explain solutions.

00:00:23.649 --> 00:00:25.406 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:25.626 --> 00:00:28.963 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can identify the known quantities and the question.

00:00:29.183 --> 00:00:32.048 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can use a bar model to choose an operation.

00:00:32.268 --> 00:00:35.567 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can give an answer with its unit and check its size.

00:00:36.217 --> 00:00:39.718 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First understand what is known and what is being asked.

00:00:39.938 --> 00:00:44.782 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A total combines parts; a difference compares amounts or
finds what remains.

00:00:45.002 --> 00:00:49.070 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A simple bar can show those relationships before you
calculate.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:55.659 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A library has one thousand two hundred and forty-eight books
and receives three hundred and seventy-five more.

00:00:55.879 --> 00:00:59.199 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both quantities are parts of the new total, so add.

00:00:59.849 --> 00:01:03.810 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The new total is one thousand six hundred and twenty-three
books.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:07.148 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It is greater than either part, which fits the story.

00:01:07.368 --> 00:01:11.752 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rough check gives about twelve hundred plus four hundred,
or sixteen hundred.

00:01:12.402 --> 00:01:17.630 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A class needs nine hundred dollars and has saved six hundred
and seventy-five dollars.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:19.542 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How much more is needed?

00:01:20.192 --> 00:01:23.003 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This asks for the gap, so subtract.

00:01:23.653 --> 00:01:28.866 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Nine hundred minus six hundred and seventy-five is two
hundred and twenty-five dollars.

00:01:29.086 --> 00:01:33.831 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Adding the gap to the savings returns the target, so the
model and answer agree.

00:01:34.481 --> 00:01:37.108 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The word more does not always mean add.

00:01:37.328 --> 00:01:40.014 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How much more is needed asks for a gap.

00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:45.613 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Picture the savings as one part of the target, find the
missing part, and add it back to check.

00:01:46.263 --> 00:01:51.113 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A school has eight hundred and twenty seats and five hundred
and eighty-six are filled.

00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:53.018 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How many are empty?

00:01:57.018 --> 00:01:59.833 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two hundred and thirty-four seats are empty.

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:04.809 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Filled seats and empty seats combine to make all eight
hundred and twenty seats.

00:02:05.459 --> 00:02:08.628 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Understand, model, calculate, and check.

00:02:08.848 --> 00:02:12.357 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose an operation from the relationship between
quantities.

00:02:12.577 --> 00:02:16.721 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Give the answer with its unit and explain why its size makes
sense.

00:02:16.941 --> 00:02:20.100 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now choose a strategy and explain why it works.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.468 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Have a go at the lesson practice on MathMagic!
